#heading(level: 2)[#text("Mixed Miller identity — written argument")]

#text("Fix a nonzero19-torsion point T of one retained elliptic factor and choose the normalized Miller function f_​T with divisor19(T)-19(O). Let g_​A,B be the normalized line quotient with divisor(A)+(B)-(A+B)-(O), including the vertical/tangent cases by rational continuation. Put")

#block(inset: 5pt)[#set text(size: 8pt)
#raw("h_T(X,Y)=g_(T-Y,Y)(X)/(x(X)+x(Y)).", block: true)]

#text("The divisor in X is(T-Y)+(O)-(T)-(-Y), since the vertical denominator has divisor(Y)+(-Y)-2(O). The ratio f_​T(X+Y)/(f_​T(X)*f_​T(Y)) has19 times this divisor. Their quotient is constant in X. The standard local parameter at O and the monic normalization of f_​T and the line quotient make that constant1. This proves the generic rational identity")

#block(inset: 5pt)[#set text(size: 8pt)
#raw("f_T(X+Y)=f_T(Y)*h_T(X,Y)^19*f_T(X).", block: true)]

#text("It holds as a rational identity across charts. The native code stores the group/line circuit for h, checks both orientations in exact one-variable function fields, and checks80 full lift slices. Slices alone do not prove the global identity. The divisor/normalization argument is the bridge; it is not formalized in Lean.")

#text("Integer combinations of sigma(T) have explicit univariate corrections prod f_​T^weights=f_​sum*g^19. All48 class/sum identities are checked by cross-multiplying reduced rational functions on the elliptic curve. The actual graph equality D*sigma(T)=T*M, inverse matrix N and beta=B give")

#block(inset: 5pt)[#set text(size: 8pt)
#raw("H_i(P)=sum_correction_i(P3)\n  *product_(j=1..3) h_Tij(P_(j-1),-P3)\n  /product_(j=0..4) class_correction_ij(Pj).", block: true)]

#text("Consequently H_​i(P)^19 is exactly the ratio between the destination radicand at beta(P) and the signed N-combination of coefficient-Frobenius radicands at P. This proves the displayed root lift generically. All coefficients of the univariate factors, T points and rational operations are retained; sigma and beta are explicit, so H is an executable circuit.")

#heading(level: 2)[#text("Global cocycle and chart transport — written argument")]

#text("The retained independent19-torsion Picard classes give the connected, integral Kummer extension of degree19^2, as in round08's written identification with the actual quotient. Since N is invertible modulo19, the lift is a generic isomorphism over the base Frobenius. Its360-fold iterate fixes the base: B^5=1 and sigma^360=1. It is therefore an automorphism of that Kummer function field over its base. Because mu19 is contained in K, each z_​i can only acquire a constant mu19 multiplier. A fixture with both roots nonzero, and with every iteration defined, fixes both multipliers to1. Two such fixtures per graph pass. Lean proves the nonzero-root cancellation implication, not this whole geometric bridge.")

#text("Let rho_​i,k be the product of the five retained chart transition factors, with rho_​i,0=1. In source chart k and target chart l, the lifted formula is")

#block(inset: 5pt)[#set text(size: 8pt)
#raw("z'_i,l=product_j sigma(z_j,k)^N_ji * H_i(sigma(P))\n  *rho_i,l(B*sigma(P))/product_j sigma(rho_j,k(P))^N_ji.", block: true)]

#text("This is substitution and cancellation of the chart definitions. All200 old factors pass coefficient-Frobenius checks at both fixtures. The parsed checker verifies every36 chart pair, both roots and both fixtures, 576 squares total, and recomposes2,880 semilinear steps using shared field primitives. Full global identities use the written rational argument.")

#heading(level: 2)[#text("Invariant rational generators — written argument")]

#text("The exact field basis b_​k and its trace dual d_​k satisfy sum_​k d_​k*sigma^j(b_​k)=delta_​(j,0). Native arithmetic verifies all360 identities directly, in addition to129,600 trace-duality entries and64 trace controls. Let L be the retained geometrically integral quotient function field over K and rho its semilinear function action of order360. The fixed trace sum I_​(f,k)=sum_​j rho^j(b_​k*f) is invariant by cyclic reindexing. Expanding the double sum gives")

#block(inset: 5pt)[#set text(size: 8pt)
#raw("sum_k d_k*I_(f,k)\n  =sum_j (sum_k d_k*sigma^j(b_k))*rho^j(f)=f.", block: true)]

#text("Lean proves the reindexing and this double-sum implication for arbitrary finite index types; the native field/basis interpretation remains separate. Taking f to be all twelve chart coordinates shows that adjoining K to the field E generated overF2 by these invariants gives L. To identify E with L^rho, use the linear independence of the K basis after scalar extension from the invariant field: finite Galois descent gives K tensor_​F2 L^rho ≅ L as vector spaces. Expressing an invariant in L=K E uniquely in that basis forces all coefficients except the constant one to vanish. Hence L^rho=E. This is a written application of Galois field/vector-space descent, not a generated polynomial presentation.")

#text("The point controls compute5,760 invariant values for four coordinate families,16 recoveries and128 identities I(alpha(P))=I(P)^2. They are not a claim that arbitrary K-point invariant values belong toF2. The12×360 family is a rational generating family, potentially redundant. Its polynomial relation ideal, saturation, gluing of invariant affine opens and closed projective embedding are not computed.")

